<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1928 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Indonesia Raya</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>Indonesia's national anthem, first played on a violin by its composer at a youth congress in 1928, when the country was a Dutch colony.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Wage Rudolf Supratman</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Wage Rudolf Supratman</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Indonesian nationalists; Indonesia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Indonesian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Bandung and Jakarta, Indonesia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>completed 1924 in Bandung; revised and first performed 28 October 1928</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Wage Rudolf Supratman (d. 1938))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia-raya">Indonesia Raya</a>&quot; (&quot;Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>&quot;) was written by Wage Rudolf Supratman, a musician and young reporter. Living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>, he read an essay in Timbul magazine that challenged Indonesian musicians to compose a future national anthem, and he took up the challenge, finishing a song called &quot;Indonesia&quot; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a> in 1924. According to Tempo, he revised the words after reading Tan Malaka's book <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> Actie (1926), and the song went through several revisions before reaching its final form in 1928.</p><p>In 1928 young people from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> met at the Indonesian Youth Congress to press for independence. Supratman asked to report on it; the organisers, fearing that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial authorities would ban the meeting, asked him not to publish, and let him attend freely when he agreed. Encouraged by the congress leader Sugondo Djojopuspito, he played the song on the violin on 28 October 1928, during the Second Youth Congress that issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youth-pledge">Youth Pledge</a>, hoping it would one day be the national anthem. The song marked the birth of a nationalist movement for the whole archipelago.</p><p>Political and student organisations spread the text, and the press printed it: the daily Soeloeh Ra'jat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> on 7 November 1928 and the Chinese Indonesian weekly Sin Po on 10 November, which openly published both words and music in defiance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> authorities. In 1929 Supratman renamed the song &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia-raya">Indonesia Raya</a>&quot;, adding &quot;national anthem of Indonesia&quot; beneath the title, and sold a thousand printed copies to friends and family. The record executive Tio Tek Hong issued the first records, with Supratman keeping the copyright, and another businessman, Yo Kim Tjan, had a recording made abroad. The records sold well, but in 1930 the Dutch colonial authorities banned the song and seized the unsold copies. Supratman died in 1938.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> proclaimed its independence on 17 August 1945, the first stanza became the national anthem. In 1950 the government asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> arranger Jozef Cleber, then with the Cosmopolitan Orchestra sent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> to help Indonesian musical life, for a symphonic version. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> found his first recording, made in early 1951 with the orchestras of Radio Republik Indonesia, too ornate and asked for something as plain as the red and white flag and as grave as the Dutch anthem &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus</a>&quot;. Cleber, who heard something of the &quot;Marseillaise&quot; in the tune, made a second arrangement, maestoso con bravura, to which Sukarno asked him to add a gentle grazioso passage before the climax. That version was used for 47 years, until Addie MS recorded a new one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> in 1998 with his Twilite Orchestra, the Victorian Philharmonic Orchestra and the Melbourne Symphony Orchestra.</p><p>The rights passed after Supratman's death to his four sisters, but as the national anthem the song became the property of the state. The government obtained the sole rights from his family in 1958, Yo Kim Tjan handed over the original record the following year, and on 31 May 1960 each sister received 250,000 rupiah. A 1958 State Gazette limits the anthem to the first stanza, and Article 36B of the Constitution names it. It is played at flag-raising ceremonies in schools every Monday, timed so that the flag reaches the top as the music ends, and at the ceremony led by the President on Independence Day, 17 August.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> as a homeland and a single nation. It speaks of loyalty to the land and its people and looks forward to a great and free Indonesia. The mood is proud and resolute.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Indonesian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Indonesian</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Indonesia, tanah airkoe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Indonesia, my native land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Tanah toempah darahkoe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the land where my blood was shed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Disanalah akoe berdiri,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>there I stand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Mendjaga Pandoe Iboekoe.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>keeping watch, a scout of my Mother.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Indonesia kebangsaankoe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Indonesia, my nationhood,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Kebangsaan tanah airkoe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the nationhood of my native land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Marilah kita berseroe:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let us cry out:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">&quot;Indonesia Bersatoe&quot;.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Indonesia united&quot;.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Hidoeplah tanahkoe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live my land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Hidoeplah neg'rikoe,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>long live my country,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Bangsakoe, djiwakoe, semoea,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my nation, my soul, all of it,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Bangoenlah rajatnja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>awaken its people,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Bangoenlah badannja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>awaken its body,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Oentoek Indonesia Raja.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for Great Indonesia.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Indones', Indones',</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Indones', Indones',</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Moelia, Moelia,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>noble, noble,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Tanahkoe, neg'rikoe jang koetjinta.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my land, my country that I love.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Indones', Indones',</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Indones', Indones',</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Moelia, Moelia,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>noble, noble,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Hidoeplah Indonesia Raja.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>long live Great Indonesia.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 48 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Indonesia, tanah jang moelia,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Indonesia, the noble land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Tanah kita jang kaja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our rich land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Disanalah akoe hidoep,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>there I live</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Oentoek s'lama-lamanja.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for ever and ever.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Indonesia, tanah poesaka,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Indonesia, the land of our heritage,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Poesaka kita semoea,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the heritage of us all,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Marilah kita mendoa:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let us pray:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">&quot;Indonesia Bahagia&quot;.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Indonesia happy&quot;.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Soeboerlah tanahnja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May its land be fertile,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Soeboerlah djiwanja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>may its soul be fertile,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Bangsanja, rajatnja, semoeanya,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>its nation, its people, all of them,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Sedarlah hatinja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>may its heart be awake,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Sedarlah boedinja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>may its mind be awake,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Oentoek Indonesia Raja.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for Great Indonesia.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Indonesia, tanah jang soetji,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Indonesia, the holy land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Bagi kita disini,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for us here,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Disanalah kita berdiri,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>there we stand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Mendjaga Iboe sedjati.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>guarding the true Mother.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Indonesia, tanah berseri,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Indonesia, the radiant land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Tanah jang terkoetjintai,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the most beloved land,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Marilah kita berdjandji:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let us make a vow:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">&quot;Indonesia Bersatoe&quot;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Indonesia united&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">S'lamatlah rajatnja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Safe be its people,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">S'lamatlah poet'ranja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>safe be its sons,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Poelaoenja, laoetnja, semoea,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>its islands, its seas, all of them,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Madjoelah neg'rinja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>forward its country,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Madjoelah Pandoenja,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>forward its scouts,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="id">Oentoek Indonesia Raja.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for Great Indonesia.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: W. R. Supratman (1928); the original text, in the spelling of 1928. Text from <a href="https://id.wikisource.org/wiki/Indonesia_Raya" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>The 1928 spelling: oe = u, dj = j, tj = c, j = y, so 'Raja' is 'Raya' (great), not 'raja' (king); 'rajat' is 'rakyat' (the people).</li><li>line 1.2: 'tanah tumpah darahku', literally 'the land where my blood was spilled', is the usual Malay idiom for one's birthplace; it does not mean a battlefield.</li><li>line 1.4: 'Mendjaga Pandoe Iboekoe' is hard to construe ('guarding' + 'scout/guide' + 'my Mother'); read as 'keeping watch as a scout of my Mother[land]'. Later versions have 'Jadi pandu ibuku' ('to be my mother's scout'). 'Pandu' is a guide, and also the word for a Boy Scout. [?]</li><li>Mother (Ibu) throughout is the motherland.</li><li>line 2.4-2.5 and 5.4-5.5: 'Bangunlah' can mean 'awake' or 'build up'; 'Sedarlah' = be conscious, be aware; 'budi' = mind, character.</li><li>stanza 3 (refrain): 1928 has 'Moelia' (noble, glorious) where the anthem later sings 'Merdeka' (free). 'Indones'' is the name shortened for the metre, kept as written.</li><li>line 5.1-5.2: 'Suburlah' = be fertile, flourish.</li><li>line 7.4-7.5: 'Majulah' = go forward, progress.</li></ul></details></section>
